				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/the-great-hippo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3938622); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3938622&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200904" alt="The Great Hippo" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3938622)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/the-great-hippo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3938622); return false;" >The Great Hippo</a></span> <span class="odate time_1555830102 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">21 Apr 2019 07:01</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4225294">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Notably, SCP-4589 cultures do not divide as normal cultures would, instead, they continue to grow steadily and develop pseudopods, limbs, and rudimentary sensory organs.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The comma between &quot;would&quot; and &quot;instead&quot; ought to be a semicolon (otherwise, this is a comma splice).</p>
<p>This didn't work for me. The core idea is neat, but the premise alone isn't strong enough to carry the execution. Unlike, say, your <a href="/scp-3794">salsa hammer skip</a>, there's more I need to see here to make it &quot;work&quot;. It's not enough that you tell me about this fleshy mass of tissue; you need to <em>show</em> me. Either directly (via logs) or indirectly (where we somehow explore its consequences or the people involved in it).</p>
<p>It reminds me of the second half of <a href="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xenogears">Xenogears</a>. It's an old JRPG where they ran over budget mid-way through production &#8212; so the last quarter of the game consists of characters telling you about the content rather than you playing through it (I'm not even making this up; they literally sit down, look at the screen, and tell you what happened in the dungeons you never got to visit).</p>
<p>This can work when the content is a short, fun punchline or a compelling idea that stands on its own, but when it's like the image you're trying to evoke here, we need to experience it in a way that goes beyond a brief summary.</p>

							</div>

								
